# Answer 13 · The Gender Critique of Structural Adjustment Policies (CSS 2022)
@py question 2022-8

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Explain what SAPs were and why they were called gender-blind, then set out the critique point by point with evidence, the official response, Pakistan, and a verdict.
@ask DISCUSS IN DETAIL the GENDER CRITIQUE of STRUCTURAL ADJUSTMENT POLICIES (SAPs)
(1) Introduction: SAPs were IMF and World Bank loan conditions; feminists showed their costs fell on women
(2) Origins: the 1982 debt crisis and the Washington Consensus
(3) SAP conditions: spending cuts, subsidy removal, user fees, devaluation, privatisation, liberalisation
(4) Why "gender-blind": the invisible care economy; Elson's "infinitely elastic" labour
(5) Unpaid work rises: caring for the sick, stretching food and fuel
(6) Health and education: fees reduce maternal care; girls leave school first
(7) Jobs and income: public-sector cuts; push into informal work
(8) Export jobs: new work for women, but insecure and low-paid
(9) Food prices, stress and violence; men losing the breadwinner role
(10) No voice: decisions by lenders and finance ministries
(11) Evidence and response: UNICEF 1987; Commonwealth 1989; safety nets and PRSPs
(12) Pakistan: IMF programmes since 1988; the 2024 EFF; BISP as a safety net
(13) Conclusion: adjustment with a gender face
:::

## Decoding the question
**"Discuss in detail the gender critique"** asks you to set out, with evidence, **what feminists and others said was wrong** with SAPs from a gender point of view. Explain first what SAPs were, then give the critique **point by point** (each a sub-heading), then note the evidence, the official response, Pakistan's experience and a balanced verdict.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Structural Adjustment Programmes (SAPs)** were packages of economic reforms that the **IMF** and **World Bank** required from developing countries in return for loans, especially in the **1980s and 1990s**.

#### ii) The argument
SAPs were designed as if they were **gender-neutral**, but feminist economists showed that they shifted the costs of adjustment onto **women** — above all onto their **unpaid work** — while giving women no voice. The critique changed how development institutions talk about social protection, though the core policies changed less.

### 2. Origins
In ==1982== **Mexico** announced that it could not pay its debts, starting a **debt crisis** across Latin America, Africa and Asia. Lenders required reforms that the economist **John Williamson** later (==1989==) called the "**Washington Consensus**": fiscal discipline, market liberalisation and privatisation. The aim was to cut deficits, control inflation and earn foreign exchange to repay debt — goals set in Washington, not in the borrowing countries.

### 3. SAP conditions

::: table | The main SAP conditions.
@widths 4.4,9.6
Condition | What it meant
Cut government spending | Less for health, education, food subsidies and public jobs
Remove subsidies | Food, fuel and electricity prices rise
User fees | Charges for clinics and schools
Devaluation | Imported food and medicine become dearer
Privatisation | State firms sold; public jobs cut
Trade and financial liberalisation | Tariffs removed; exports promoted
Labour deregulation | Weaker job protection; "flexible" labour
:::

### 4. Why SAPs were called "gender-blind"

#### i) The invisible economy
SAPs treated the economy as **markets plus the state**. They ignored the **household**, where unpaid care keeps people alive. When the state withdrew, households had to fill the gap — and inside households the extra work fell on **women**.

#### ii) Elson's critique
**Diane Elson** (*Male Bias in the Development Process*, ==1991==) argued that adjustment policies contain **male bias**: they assume women's unpaid labour can expand without limit — as if it were "**infinitely elastic**" — to absorb the costs of cuts.

### 5. Unpaid work rises
When hospitals charged fees or discharged patients early, **women cared for the sick at home**. When subsidies ended, women spent more time **stretching food and fuel**, walking farther for cheaper goods, and queuing. The saving to the state was paid for with **women's time**. Because that time is unpaid and uncounted, official statistics showed the savings but not the cost — a gap that feminist economists set out to measure through **time-use surveys**.

### 6. Health and education

#### i) Women's health
Health spending cuts and **user fees** reduced women's use of **maternal health** services; in some countries maternal deaths rose. Women also delayed treatment for themselves so that children or husbands could be treated first.

#### ii) Girls' schooling
School fees and household poverty led families to **withdraw girls** first — to save money and to help with extra housework.

### 7. Jobs and income

#### i) Public-sector cuts
Cuts removed jobs in **teaching, nursing and administration**, where many women were employed.

#### ii) Informal work
To make up lost income, women moved into **informal work** — petty trade, home-based production, domestic service — with low pay and no protection. Their earnings kept households afloat but were invisible in official accounts.

### 8. Export jobs
Export promotion created jobs for women in **garments, electronics and flowers**. But they were often **low-paid, insecure and without labour rights**, in export zones where unions were discouraged. Adjustment used women's cheap labour to earn foreign exchange. Women gained income, but on terms that left them without security when orders fell.

::: fig flow | How structural adjustment shifted costs onto women.
@per 3
Cuts and user fees: services shrink
Subsidies removed: prices rise
Public jobs cut: incomes fall
Households fill the gap
Women's unpaid work and informal work rise
Girls leave school; women's health suffers
:::

### 9. Food prices, stress and violence
Removal of food subsidies hit poor women responsible for feeding families. Economic stress was linked with rising **domestic tension and violence**. Where men lost jobs and the **breadwinner** role, more of the burden of survival fell on women, who often combined several informal jobs with all the housework.

### 10. No voice
SAPs were negotiated by **finance ministries** and lenders. Women — and the social ministries that served them — had **no say** in decisions that reshaped their lives. Budgets were judged by deficits and growth, never by their effect on women's time, health or income.

### 11. The evidence and the response

#### i) Evidence
**UNICEF**'s *Adjustment with a Human Face* (**Cornia, Jolly and Stewart**, ==1987==) documented harm to children and women. The **Commonwealth Secretariat**'s *Engendering Adjustment for the 1990s* (==1989==) showed the burden on women. **DAWN** (==1985==) put the debt crisis at the centre of its critique.

#### ii) Response
The IMF and World Bank added **social safety nets**, then **Poverty Reduction Strategy Papers** (==1999==) and gender language. Critics argue that the **core conditions** — austerity, liberalisation — changed little, and that safety nets treat women as vulnerable recipients rather than as citizens with rights to public services.

### 12. The Pakistan angle

#### i) Adjustment in Pakistan
Pakistan's first IMF structural adjustment programme began in ==1988==; many followed, including a **Stand-By Arrangement** (==2023==) and a **7-billion-dollar Extended Fund Facility** (==2024==). Typical conditions include higher **electricity and gas prices**, wider **sales tax** and restrained development spending.

#### ii) Gendered effects
Women who manage household budgets feel **price rises** first; pressure on **health and education** increases their unpaid care; and informal work grows. Sales tax on essentials falls heavily on poor households, while cuts to development spending delay water schemes and clinics that save women's time.

#### iii) Protection
The **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==), which pays cash to women, has been **expanded** during IMF programmes — a partial answer to the gender critique, though cash cannot replace public services.

### 13. Conclusion
The gender critique showed that SAPs were not neutral: they **transferred the costs** of adjustment from the state to **women's unpaid labour**, harmed women's health and girls' schooling, pushed women into insecure work, and excluded them from decisions. The critique does not deny that economies sometimes need stabilisation; it insists that stabilisation must be **gender-assessed** — protecting health, education and social protection, using progressive taxes and counting unpaid care. In UNICEF's phrase, adjustment needs a **human face** — and that face is very often a woman's.

## Why this answer scores
- Explains **SAPs** and the idea of **gender blindness** before the critique.
- The critique is set out **point by point**, each with a sub-heading.
- **Evidence** (Elson, UNICEF, Commonwealth, DAWN) and the **official response** make it detailed and balanced.
- A **table**, a **flow figure** and a **Pakistan** section (1988 to 2024; BISP) complete it.
